# Shell scripts must stay LF on every platform: the served install.sh bytes have
# to match the published SHA256, and CRLF would also break `#!/bin/sh` execution.
*.sh text eol=lf
install.sh text eol=lf
# The published checksum is consumed by `sha256sum -c`, which is sensitive to a
# trailing CR; keep it LF too.
*.sha256 text eol=lf
# The frozen crypto vectors: the canonical copy and the copy published at
# /interop/crypto-v1.json must be byte-identical, and tests/vectors.test.ts
# compares raw bytes. Under core.autocrlf=true a Windows checkout would rewrite
# both to CRLF and the generator would then rewrite them to LF — keep git out
# of it entirely.
vectors/crypto-v1.json text eol=lf
apps/broker/public/interop/crypto-v1.json text eol=lf
